WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.860
Bash scripts often exist to invoke other programs.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:16.360
They call backup tools, deployment clients, package managers, cloud CLIs, database utilities, file processors, monitoring tools, test runners, and administrative commands.

3
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:25.740
In many organizations, Bash is the glue between scheduled jobs, CI/CD systems, release workflows, and operational maintenance.

4
00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.820
Every process invocation is a boundary.

5
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:40.200
The script decides which executable runs, what arguments it receives, what environment it inherits, where it runs, what it can read or write, and how its result will be interpreted.

6
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.300
Those choices are security choices, reliability choices, and operations choices at the same time.

7
00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:56.820
Small invocation mistakes can have large consequences because the invoked program may touch real files, services, credentials, deployment targets, or production data.

8
00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:11.000
A wrong executable, wrong working directory, malformed argument, leaked environment variable, hidden error stream, or ignored exit status can turn a routine script into an incident or a difficult troubleshooting exercise.

9
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:18.400
Safe process invocation is the discipline of making those decisions explicit before automation reaches production.

10
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.140
The defensive goal is not to make Bash complicated.

11
00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:30.280
The goal is to make process execution predictable, reviewable, and controlled enough that another engineer can understand what will run and what assumptions must be true.

12
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.520
In this course, we will treat process invocation as an engineering interface.

13
00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:49.620
We will examine executable selection, argument passing, environment control, working directory assumptions, file and stream handling, exit status, timeouts, privileges, logging, review, and tests.

14
00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.720
The result is a practical checklist for safer Bash automation.

15
00:01:53.820 --> 00:02:02.940
The result is process execution predictable and controlled enough that another engineer can verify what will run and which assumptions must hold.
